<div class="page" id="page-topic1">
  <div class="section-title">🚀 <span>Topic 1: Motion, Forces &amp; Energy</span></div>
  <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag tag-amber">Paper 2 Hot Topic</span><span class="tag tag-pink">Calculation Heavy</span><span class="tag tag-blue">Graph Interpretation</span></div>
  <div class="notes-container">

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.1 Physical Quantities &amp; Measurement <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Scalar</strong>Has magnitude only. Examples: distance, speed, time, mass, energy, temperature.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Vector</strong>Has magnitude AND direction. Examples: force, weight, velocity, acceleration, momentum, electric field strength, gravitational field strength.</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Measurement Techniques</h4>
          <ul><li>Rulers and measuring cylinders → length and volume</li>
          <li>Clocks and digital timers → time intervals</li>
          <li>Determine average: measure multiples (e.g., time 10 pendulum oscillations ÷ 10 = period)</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Resultant of two perpendicular vectors:</strong> Use Pythagoras: R = √(A² + B²). Angle θ = tan⁻¹(B/A). This applies to forces and velocities only.</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> "A car travels north at 60 km/h" → velocity. "A car travels at 60 km/h" → speed. Velocity MUST have direction.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.2 Motion ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Speed</strong>Distance travelled per unit time. v = s/t. Scalar. Unit: m/s</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Velocity</strong>Speed in a given direction. Vector. Unit: m/s</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Acceleration</strong>Change in velocity per unit time. a = Δv/Δt. Unit: m/s²</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Speed</span>v = s / t</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Average speed</span>v = total distance / total time</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Acceleration</span>a = Δv / Δt</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Free fall</span>g ≈ 9.8 m/s²</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Distance–Time Graph</h4>
          <ul><li>Horizontal line → object at rest</li>
          <li>Straight diagonal line → constant speed (gradient = speed)</li>
          <li>Curve getting steeper → accelerating</li>
          <li>Curve getting shallower → decelerating</li></ul>
          <h4>Speed–Time Graph</h4>
          <ul><li>Horizontal line → constant speed (zero acceleration)</li>
          <li>Straight line sloping up → constant acceleration (gradient = acceleration)</li>
          <li>Curve → changing acceleration</li>
          <li>Area under graph = distance travelled</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Terminal Velocity:</strong> Object falling through fluid → weight > drag → accelerates. As speed ↑, drag ↑. Eventually drag = weight → terminal velocity (zero acceleration, constant speed). Removing medium (e.g., parachute opening) causes deceleration then new terminal velocity.</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> Deceleration is negative acceleration. If asked to calculate force during deceleration, use a negative value for a in F = ma.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.3 Mass &amp; Weight <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Mass</strong>Measure of quantity of matter. Constant everywhere. Unit: kg.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Weight</strong>Gravitational force on a mass: W = mg. Changes with location. Unit: N.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Gravitational field strength (g)</strong>Force per unit mass: g = W/m. On Earth ≈ 9.8 N/kg. Equivalent to acceleration of free fall.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Weight</span>W = mg</div>
        <div class="warn-box"><strong>Key distinction:</strong> Mass is constant everywhere. Weight = 0 in zero gravity. A 10 kg object weighs 98 N on Earth but only ~16 N on the Moon (g_moon ≈ 1.6 N/kg).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.4 Density <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Density (ρ)</strong>Mass per unit volume. ρ = m/V. Units: kg/m³ or g/cm³.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Density</span>ρ = m / V</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Measuring Density</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Regular solid:</strong> measure dimensions, calculate volume, weigh on balance</li>
          <li><strong>Liquid:</strong> measure volume in measuring cylinder, weigh before and after adding</li>
          <li><strong>Irregular solid:</strong> displacement — submerge in water, volume displaced = object's volume</li></ul>
          <p>Object floats if ρ_object &lt; ρ_fluid. Sinks if ρ_object &gt; ρ_fluid.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement:</strong> Oil floats on water because ρ_oil &lt; ρ_water and the liquids do not mix. If liquid A has lower density than liquid B, A floats on B.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.5 Forces ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Newton's 1st Law</strong>Object remains at rest OR constant velocity unless acted on by a resultant force.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Newton's 2nd Law (Supplement)</strong>F = ma. Resultant force and acceleration are in the same direction.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Spring constant (Supplement)</strong>Force per unit extension: k = F/x. Unit: N/m. Applies up to limit of proportionality only.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Newton's 2nd</span>F = ma</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Spring constant</span>k = F / x</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Moment of a force</span>M = F × d  (d = perp. distance from pivot)</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Friction</h4>
          <ul><li>Solid friction: between two surfaces, impedes motion, produces heat</li>
          <li>Drag: friction in liquids or gases acting on moving objects</li></ul>
          <h4>Moments</h4>
          <ul><li>Principle of moments: clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments (at equilibrium)</li>
          <li>Equilibrium: no resultant force AND no resultant moment</li></ul>
          <h4>Centre of Gravity</h4>
          <ul><li>Point where weight of object acts</li>
          <li>Lower CoG → more stable. Wider base → more stable.</li>
          <li>Found experimentally using plumb line and card shape</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Circular Motion:</strong> Force perpendicular to velocity. If force ↑ (mass &amp; radius const) → speed ↑. If force ↑ (mass &amp; speed const) → radius ↓. More mass needs more force to keep r and v constant.</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> Hooke's Law (F ∝ x) only holds BELOW the limit of proportionality. Above it, the graph curves — do not use k = F/x beyond that point.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.6 Momentum (Supplement) <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Momentum (p)</strong>p = mv. Vector. Unit: kg m/s.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Impulse</strong>Force × time = change in momentum. impulse = FΔt = Δ(mv). Unit: N s.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Momentum</span>p = mv</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Impulse</span>FΔt = Δ(mv)</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Resultant force</span>F = Δp / Δt</div>
        <div class="tip-box"><strong>Conservation of Momentum:</strong> In a closed system, total momentum before = total momentum after a collision or explosion. Always assign directions as + or –.</div>
        <div class="warn-box"><strong>Common error:</strong> In explosions starting from rest, total initial momentum = 0. So m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ = 0, meaning objects move in opposite directions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.7 Energy, Work &amp; Power ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Energy Stores</h4>
          <p>Kinetic, gravitational potential, chemical, elastic (strain), nuclear, electrostatic, internal (thermal).</p>
          <h4>Energy Transfers</h4>
          <p>By: forces (mechanical work), electrical currents, heating, electromagnetic/sound waves.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Kinetic energy</span>Eₖ = ½mv²</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Gravitational PE</span>ΔEₚ = mgΔh</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Work done</span>W = Fd = ΔE</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Power</span>P = W/t = ΔE/t</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Efficiency (%)</span>(useful output / total input) × 100%</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Energy Resources</h4>
          <ul><li>Renewable: solar, wind, hydro, tidal, wave, geothermal, biofuels</li>
          <li>Non-renewable: fossil fuels, nuclear fuel</li>
          <li>Sun = primary source of most energy except geothermal, nuclear, tidal</li>
          <li>Nuclear fusion in Sun releases energy; research ongoing for fusion power on Earth</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Sankey diagrams:</strong> Width of arrow ∝ energy. Useful energy goes in main direction; wasted energy shown as branching arrows (usually heat). Sum of all output arrows = total input.</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> Eₖ = ½mv² — double the speed gives FOUR times the kinetic energy (v is squared!). This is why car braking distances quadruple when speed doubles.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">1.8 Pressure <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Pressure</strong>Force per unit area. p = F/A. Unit: N/m² = Pa.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Pressure</span>p = F / A</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Liquid pressure (Supp)</span>Δp = ρgΔh</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <p>Pressure in liquids increases with depth and density. Acts equally in all directions at a given depth.</p>
          <h4>Everyday Examples</h4>
          <ul><li>Wide tyres → large area → reduced pressure on ground</li>
          <li>Sharp knife → tiny area → high pressure to cut</li>
          <li>Snowshoes → large area → reduced pressure to avoid sinking</li>
          <li>Hydraulic systems: pressure transmitted equally throughout liquid</li></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- TOPIC 2 -->
<div class="page" id="page-topic2">
  <div class="section-title">🌡 <span>Topic 2: Thermal Physics</span></div>
  <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag tag-blue">Kinetic Model</span><span class="tag tag-green">SHC Calculations</span><span class="tag tag-amber">Gas Laws</span></div>
  <div class="notes-container">
    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">2.1 Kinetic Particle Model ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>States of Matter</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Solid:</strong> particles close, regular arrangement, vibrate about fixed positions, strong forces</li>
          <li><strong>Liquid:</strong> particles close but irregular, can flow, weaker forces</li>
          <li><strong>Gas:</strong> particles far apart, random rapid motion, very weak forces, fill container</li></ul>
          <h4>State Changes</h4>
          <p>Melting (solid→liquid), Boiling/Evaporation (liquid→gas), Condensation (gas→liquid), Freezing/Solidification (liquid→solid). Gas↔solid not required.</p>
          <h4>Brownian Motion</h4>
          <p>Random zigzag movement of microscopic particles (e.g. smoke in air). Evidence for the kinetic particle model — caused by random collisions with fast-moving gas molecules.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Absolute Zero</strong>–273°C = 0 K. Lowest possible temperature — particles have minimum kinetic energy.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Temperature</span>T (K) = θ (°C) + 273</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Gas Pressure (Kinetic Theory)</h4>
          <ul><li>Gas exerts pressure due to particles colliding with container walls (force per unit area)</li>
          <li>Temperature ↑ at constant volume → particles faster → more/harder collisions → pressure ↑</li>
          <li>Volume ↓ at constant temperature → collisions more frequent → pressure ↑</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Boyle's Law:</strong> pV = constant at fixed temperature and fixed mass. If p doubles, V halves. Use: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap #1:</strong> ALWAYS convert °C to Kelvin before gas law calculations! 0°C ≠ 0 K.<br><strong>MCQ Trap #2:</strong> Brownian motion shows evidence for MOLECULES (not atoms). Don't say the pollen/smoke particles are the molecules.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">2.2 Thermal Properties &amp; Temperature <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Thermal Expansion</h4>
          <ul><li>All materials expand when heated (at constant pressure)</li>
          <li>Order of expansion: gases &gt; liquids &gt; solids</li>
          <li>Explained by: particles vibrate more → move further apart (solids); particles more free to move (liquids/gases)</li></ul>
          <h4>Applications of Thermal Expansion</h4>
          <ul><li>Gaps in railway tracks and bridges (prevent buckling)</li>
          <li>Bimetallic strip (different expansion → strip bends → used in thermostats)</li>
          <li>Liquid thermometers (liquid expands up tube)</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Specific Heat Capacity (c)</strong>Energy required per unit mass per unit temperature increase. c = ΔE/(mΔθ). Unit: J/(kg°C) or J/(kg K).</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">SHC equation</span>ΔE = mcΔθ</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement:</strong> Temperature increase = increase in average kinetic energy of all particles. Internal energy = sum of KE + PE of all particles. Higher temperature → more average KE (not just faster speed — statistical average).</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Melting, Boiling, Evaporation</h4>
          <ul><li>Melting at 0°C (water at standard pressure): energy in, temp constant, bonds breaking</li>
          <li>Boiling at 100°C (water at standard pressure): energy in, temp constant, throughout liquid</li>
          <li>Evaporation: surface only, any temperature below b.p., more energetic molecules escape</li></ul>
          <h4>Boiling vs Evaporation</h4>
          <ul><li>Boiling: fixed temperature, throughout liquid, vigorous, bubbles form</li>
          <li>Evaporation: any temperature, surface only, slow, no bubbles</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="tip-box"><strong>Evaporation causes cooling:</strong> More energetic particles escape → average KE of remaining liquid decreases → temperature falls. Explains sweating, refrigeration, ether on skin.</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Factors affecting evaporation rate:</strong> Higher temperature, larger surface area, wind movement over surface all INCREASE rate of evaporation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">2.3 Transfer of Thermal Energy ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Conduction</h4>
          <ul><li>Main method in solids</li>
          <li>Metals: free (delocalised) electrons carry energy quickly → good conductors</li>
          <li>Non-metals: lattice vibrations only → poorer conductors</li>
          <li>Gases and most liquids: particles far apart → poor conductors (thermal insulators)</li></ul>
          <h4>Convection</h4>
          <ul><li>Occurs in liquids and gases only (not solids)</li>
          <li>Warm fluid → less dense → rises; cool fluid → more dense → sinks → convection current</li></ul>
          <h4>Radiation (Infrared)</h4>
          <ul><li>All objects emit infrared radiation; no medium required (works in vacuum)</li>
          <li>Dark, dull (matt) surfaces: best emitters AND best absorbers</li>
          <li>Light, shiny surfaces: best reflectors, worst emitters/absorbers</li>
          <li>Rate of emission depends on surface temperature and surface area</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Earth's temperature:</strong> Earth is in thermal equilibrium when energy absorbed from Sun = energy radiated away. Greenhouse gases (CO₂, H₂O vapour) absorb and re-emit infrared → Earth warms. Increased greenhouse gases → temperature rise.</div>
        <div class="warn-box"><strong>Exam Question — Vacuum flask:</strong> Mention: (1) vacuum between walls stops conduction and convection; (2) silvered/shiny surfaces reflect infrared → reduces radiation loss; (3) stopper reduces convection from top.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- TOPIC 3 -->
<div class="page" id="page-topic3">
  <div class="section-title">🌊 <span>Topic 3: Waves</span></div>
  <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag tag-amber">High Yield</span><span class="tag tag-blue">Ray Diagrams</span><span class="tag tag-green">EM Spectrum</span></div>
  <div class="notes-container">
    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">3.1 General Properties of Waves ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Wave</strong>A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter.</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Wave Properties</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Wavelength (λ):</strong> distance between two adjacent crests (or identical points)</li>
          <li><strong>Frequency (f):</strong> number of complete oscillations per second. Unit: Hz</li>
          <li><strong>Amplitude:</strong> maximum displacement from equilibrium position</li>
          <li><strong>Wave speed (v):</strong> distance travelled per unit time</li>
          <li><strong>Wavefront:</strong> line joining points of the same phase</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Wave equation</span>v = fλ</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Transverse vs Longitudinal</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Transverse:</strong> vibration ⊥ (perpendicular) to direction of propagation. Examples: EM waves, water waves, seismic S-waves.</li>
          <li><strong>Longitudinal:</strong> vibration ∥ (parallel) to direction of propagation. Examples: sound, seismic P-waves. Has compressions (high pressure) and rarefactions (low pressure).</li></ul>
          <h4>Wave Behaviours</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Reflection:</strong> wave bounces off surface; angle of incidence = angle of reflection (from normal)</li>
          <li><strong>Refraction:</strong> speed changes when entering new medium → direction changes</li>
          <li><strong>Diffraction:</strong> spreading of waves through gap or around edge. Greatest when gap size ≈ wavelength.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Diffraction:</strong> Longer wavelength → greater diffraction. Smaller gap → greater diffraction. If gap much larger than λ → almost no diffraction. This explains why radio waves (long λ) diffract around hills, but visible light (short λ) does not.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">3.2 Light — Reflection, Refraction &amp; Lenses ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Reflection</h4>
          <ul><li>Law: angle of incidence (i) = angle of reflection (r) — both measured from the NORMAL</li>
          <li>Plane mirror image: same size, same distance behind mirror, virtual, laterally inverted</li></ul>
          <h4>Refraction</h4>
          <ul><li>Light entering denser medium: slows down, bends TOWARDS normal</li>
          <li>Light entering less dense medium: speeds up, bends AWAY from normal</li>
          <li>Critical angle (c): when i = c in denser medium, refracted ray travels along boundary (r = 90°)</li>
          <li>Total Internal Reflection (TIR): when i &gt; c in denser medium, all light reflects internally</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Snell's Law (Supp)</span>n = sin i / sin r</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Critical angle (Supp)</span>n = 1 / sin c</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Applications of TIR</h4>
          <ul><li>Optical fibres: light travels long distances with minimal loss by TIR; used in telecommunications and endoscopes</li>
          <li>Bicycle reflectors, periscopes using prisms</li></ul>
          <h4>Thin Lenses</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Converging lens:</strong> brings parallel rays to focal point F; focal length f is positive</li>
          <li><strong>Diverging lens:</strong> spreads parallel rays; appear to come from virtual focal point</li>
          <li>Real image: formed by actual converging rays; can be projected on screen; always inverted</li>
          <li>Virtual image: rays appear to diverge from this point; cannot be projected; upright</li></ul>
          <h4>Image Characteristics Summary</h4>
          <ul><li>Object beyond 2F: real, inverted, diminished</li>
          <li>Object at 2F: real, inverted, same size</li>
          <li>Object between F and 2F: real, inverted, enlarged</li>
          <li>Object inside F: virtual, upright, enlarged (magnifying glass)</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Eye defects:</strong> Short sight (myopia): image forms in front of retina → correct with diverging lens. Long sight (hyperopia): image forms behind retina → correct with converging lens.</div>
        <div class="tip-box"><strong>Dispersion:</strong> White light → prism → spectrum. Violet deviates most (highest n, highest f, shortest λ). Red deviates least. Order: Red Orange Yellow Green Blue Indigo Violet (ROY G BIV).</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> Angles are ALWAYS measured from the NORMAL (perpendicular to surface), not from the surface itself. Drawing the normal first is essential.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">3.3 Electromagnetic Spectrum ★ MUST KNOW <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>EM Spectrum — Low to High Frequency</h4>
          <p style="font-family:monospace;color:var(--amber)">Radio → Microwave → Infrared → Visible → UV → X-rays → Gamma</p>
          <p>All EM waves: travel at 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum, are transverse, require no medium.</p>
          <h4>Uses of Each Region</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Radio:</strong> TV/radio broadcasts, astronomy, RFID</li>
          <li><strong>Microwave:</strong> satellite TV, mobile phones, microwave ovens</li>
          <li><strong>Infrared:</strong> electric grills, remote controls, thermal imaging, optical fibres</li>
          <li><strong>Visible:</strong> vision, photography, illumination</li>
          <li><strong>UV:</strong> security marking, detecting fake banknotes, sterilising water</li>
          <li><strong>X-rays:</strong> medical scanning (bones), airport security</li>
          <li><strong>Gamma:</strong> sterilising food/medical equipment, cancer treatment/detection</li></ul>
          <h4>Harmful Effects</h4>
          <ul><li>Microwaves: internal heating of body cells</li>
          <li>Infrared: skin burns</li>
          <li>UV: skin cancer, eye damage (cataracts)</li>
          <li>X-rays/Gamma: cell mutation, cancer</li></ul>
          <h4>Satellite Communications</h4>
          <ul><li>Low orbit satellites: some satellite phones</li>
          <li>Geostationary satellites: satellite TV, satellite phones, appear fixed in sky</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Digital vs Analogue:</strong> Digital: signal is 0 or 1 (on/off). Analogue: continuously varying signal. Digital benefits: higher data rate, accurate signal regeneration (noise removed), longer range, more information per second. Mobile phones use microwaves; Bluetooth uses radio waves; optical fibres use visible/infrared light.</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> Gamma rays come from the NUCLEUS. X-rays come from electron transitions / X-ray tube. Both are ionising but different origins. Don't confuse.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">3.4 Sound <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Sound</strong>Longitudinal wave. Requires a medium (cannot travel through vacuum). Audible range: 20 Hz – 20,000 Hz. Speed in air ≈ 330–350 m/s.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Ultrasound</strong>Frequency &gt; 20 kHz. Uses: medical imaging (soft tissue), sonar, non-destructive testing of materials.</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <ul><li>Amplitude ↑ → louder sound (greater volume)</li>
          <li>Frequency ↑ → higher pitch</li>
          <li>Echo = reflection of sound; used to find distances</li>
          <li>Sound travels faster in solids than liquids than gases</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Echo distance</span>d = v × t/2</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Compressions &amp; Rarefactions:</strong> In a longitudinal sound wave, regions where particles are pushed together = compressions (high pressure). Regions where particles are spread apart = rarefactions (low pressure).</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<!-- TOPIC 4 -->
<div class="page" id="page-topic4">
  <div class="section-title">⚡ <span>Topic 4: Electricity &amp; Magnetism</span></div>
  <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag tag-pink">Calc Heavy</span><span class="tag tag-amber">Circuit Diagrams</span><span class="tag tag-blue">Transformers</span></div>
  <div class="notes-container">
    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">4.1 Magnetism <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <ul><li>Like poles repel; unlike poles attract</li>
          <li>Temporary magnets: soft iron — easy to magnetise/demagnetise</li>
          <li>Permanent magnets: steel — hard to magnetise/demagnetise, retain magnetism</li>
          <li>Magnetic field: region in which a magnetic pole experiences a force</li>
          <li>Field direction: from N pole to S pole (outside magnet)</li>
          <li>Closer field lines = stronger field</li>
          <li>Compass: N pole points in direction of magnetic field</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement:</strong> Magnetic forces are due to interactions between magnetic fields. Relative strength represented by spacing of field lines — closer together = stronger field.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">4.2 Electrical Quantities ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Electric current (I)</strong>Charge passing a point per unit time. I = Q/t. Unit: A. Conventional current: positive to negative. Electron flow: negative to positive.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>e.m.f. (E)</strong>Electrical work done by source per unit charge around complete circuit. E = W/Q. Unit: V.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Potential difference (V)</strong>Work done by unit charge passing through a component. V = W/Q. Unit: V.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Resistance (R)</strong>R = V/I. Unit: Ω (ohm). Ratio of p.d. to current.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Current</span>I = Q / t</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Resistance</span>R = V / I</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Electrical power</span>P = IV</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Electrical energy</span>E = IVt</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Power (Supp)</span>P = I²R</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>I-V Characteristics</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Ohmic resistor:</strong> straight line through origin (R = constant)</li>
          <li><strong>Filament lamp:</strong> curve — R increases as temperature increases (less steep at higher I)</li>
          <li><strong>Diode:</strong> allows current in forward direction only; very high resistance in reverse</li></ul>
          <h4>Resistance and Wire</h4>
          <ul><li>R ∝ length (double length → double resistance)</li>
          <li>R ∝ 1/cross-sectional area (thicker wire → less resistance)</li></ul>
          <h4>Special Components</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>LDR:</strong> resistance ↓ as light intensity ↑</li>
          <li><strong>Thermistor (NTC):</strong> resistance ↓ as temperature ↑</li>
          <li><strong>LED:</strong> emits light when current flows in forward direction</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> e.m.f. and p.d. both measured in volts but are NOT the same! e.m.f. is energy given to charges; p.d. is energy taken from charges as they pass through a component.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">4.3 Electric Circuits ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Series Circuits</h4>
          <ul><li>Same current throughout: I₁ = I₂ = I₃</li>
          <li>Total p.d. = sum: V = V₁ + V₂ + V₃</li>
          <li>Total resistance: R = R₁ + R₂ + R₃</li></ul>
          <h4>Parallel Circuits</h4>
          <ul><li>Same p.d. across each branch</li>
          <li>Total current = sum of branch currents: I = I₁ + I₂</li>
          <li>Combined resistance LESS than smallest individual</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Parallel resistance</span>1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Potential divider (Supp)</span>R₁/R₂ = V₁/V₂</div>
        <div class="warn-box"><strong>Advantages of parallel lamps:</strong> Each lamp works at full voltage; if one fails, others stay on; each can be switched independently.</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Kirchhoff's Laws:</strong> (1) Sum of currents into a junction = sum out. (2) Total p.d. across series components = sum of individual p.d.s. (3) p.d. across parallel branches is equal.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">4.4 Electrical Safety <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <ul><li>Mains circuit: live (brown), neutral (blue), earth (green/yellow)</li>
          <li>Switch on LIVE wire: when off, appliance disconnected from high voltage</li>
          <li>Fuse: thin wire melts if current too high → breaks circuit → protects wiring</li>
          <li>Earth wire: connects metal casing to earth; if fault occurs, current flows safely to earth → fuse blows</li>
          <li>Double insulated: no metal casing → no earth needed; use fuse to protect circuit</li>
          <li>RCD/Trip switch: detects difference in live/neutral current → switches off instantly</li></ul>
          <h4>Hazards</h4>
          <ul><li>Damaged insulation (shock risk)</li>
          <li>Overheating cables (fire risk)</li>
          <li>Damp conditions (current through body)</li>
          <li>Overloading sockets (overheating)</li></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">4.5 Electromagnetic Effects ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Electromagnetic Induction</strong>A conductor moving through a magnetic field, OR a changing magnetic field through a conductor, induces an e.m.f.</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Factors Increasing Induced e.m.f.</h4>
          <ul><li>Greater speed of movement</li>
          <li>Stronger magnetic field</li>
          <li>More turns on coil</li>
          <li>Reversing movement or field direction → e.m.f. reverses</li></ul>
          <h4>The Transformer</h4>
          <ul><li>Works only with a.c. (needs changing magnetic field)</li>
          <li>Step-up: Ns &gt; Np → Vs &gt; Vp (voltage increases, current decreases)</li>
          <li>Step-down: Ns &lt; Np → Vs &lt; Vp (voltage decreases, current increases)</li>
          <li>High voltage transmission reduces current → reduces I²R power losses in cables</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Transformer ratio</span>Vp/Vs = Np/Ns</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">100% efficiency</span>IpVp = IsVs</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Cable loss (Supp)</span>P = I²R</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Lenz's Law:</strong> Induced e.m.f. opposes the change causing it (conservation of energy). The induced current creates a magnetic field that opposes the motion.</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — d.c. Motor:</strong> Split-ring commutator reverses current direction every half turn → coil rotates continuously. Turning force increases with: more turns, higher current, stronger field.</div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> Transformers ONLY work with a.c. — NOT d.c. A steady d.c. produces no changing field → no induction → no output voltage.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

