<div class="page" id="page-topic5">
  <div class="section-title">☢ <span>Topic 5: Nuclear Physics</span></div>
  <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag tag-green">Manageable</span><span class="tag tag-amber">Key Definitions</span><span class="tag tag-blue">Half-life Calculations</span></div>
  <div class="notes-container">
    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">5.1 Nuclear Model of the Atom <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <ul><li>Atom: tiny dense positive nucleus surrounded by mostly empty space; electrons orbit nucleus</li>
          <li>Nucleus: protons (positive) + neutrons (neutral)</li>
          <li>Electrons: negative charge, orbit nucleus</li>
          <li>Positive ion: atom loses electrons. Negative ion: atom gains electrons.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Proton number (Z)</strong>Number of protons. Determines the element. Also = number of electrons in neutral atom.</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Nucleon number (A)</strong>Total number of protons + neutrons (mass number).</div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Isotope</strong>Same element (same Z), different number of neutrons (different A). Same chemical properties, different physical properties.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Neutrons</span>N = A – Z</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Rutherford Scattering:</strong> α particles fired at thin gold foil. Most pass straight through (atom mostly empty space). Small number deflect at large angles (nucleus is small, dense, positive). Very few bounce back (nucleus has high positive charge). Evidence for nuclear model of atom.</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Fission &amp; Fusion:</strong> Fission: large nucleus splits → two smaller nuclei + neutrons + energy (A decreases). Fusion: small nuclei join → larger nucleus + energy (e.g., H + H → He in Sun). Both release energy. Mass decreases → energy released.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">5.2 Radioactivity ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Background radiation</strong>Radiation always present from natural sources: radon gas (in air), rocks/buildings, food/drink, cosmic rays.</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>The Three Types of Nuclear Radiation</h4>
        </div>
        <table class="rad-table">
          <tr><th>Property</th><th style="color:var(--red)">Alpha (α)</th><th style="color:var(--blue)">Beta (β⁻)</th><th style="color:var(--green)">Gamma (γ)</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Nature</td><td>Helium nucleus ⁴₂He</td><td>Fast electron</td><td>EM radiation (photon)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Charge</td><td>+2</td><td>−1</td><td>0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mass</td><td>4 u</td><td>~0</td><td>0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ionising power</td><td>Strongest</td><td>Medium</td><td>Weakest</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Penetrating</td><td>Weakest (paper)</td><td>Medium (few mm Al)</td><td>Strongest (cm of lead)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Speed</td><td>~5% c</td><td>~90% c</td><td>c (3×10⁸ m/s)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>In E-field</td><td>Deflects towards –ve</td><td>Deflects towards +ve</td><td>No deflection</td></tr>
          <tr><td>In B-field</td><td>Deflects (opposite to β)</td><td>Deflects</td><td>No deflection</td></tr>
        </table>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Radioactive Decay — Effect on Nucleus</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>α-decay:</strong> A decreases by 4, Z decreases by 2. Emits ⁴₂He</li>
          <li><strong>β⁻-decay:</strong> A unchanged, Z increases by 1. Neutron → proton + electron. Emits ⁰₋₁e</li>
          <li><strong>γ-emission:</strong> No change in A or Z. Just energy (electromagnetic radiation)</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="def-box"><strong>Half-life</strong>Time for half the nuclei of that isotope in any sample to decay. Constant for a given isotope. Decay is spontaneous and random.</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">After n half-lives</span>N = N₀ × (½)ⁿ</div>
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>Applications — Choosing the Right Isotope</h4>
          <ul><li>Smoke alarms: Am-241, α emitter, long half-life (~432 yr) — ionises air in detector</li>
          <li>Medical tracers: Tc-99m, γ emitter, short half-life (~6 h) — detected outside body</li>
          <li>Food sterilisation: γ emitter, long half-life — kills bacteria without heating</li>
          <li>Equipment sterilisation: γ emitter</li>
          <li>Thickness gauge: β emitter — absorbed by varying thickness of material</li>
          <li>Cancer treatment: γ rays targeted at tumour</li></ul>
          <h4>Safety Precautions</h4>
          <ul><li>Reduce exposure time</li>
          <li>Increase distance from source (inverse square law)</li>
          <li>Use shielding: paper/skin (α), aluminium (β), thick lead/concrete (γ)</li>
          <li>Store in lead-lined containers; handle with tongs</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="trap-box"><strong>MCQ Trap:</strong> In Supplement calculations, ALWAYS subtract background radiation before calculating half-life: corrected count rate = measured – background.</div>
        <div class="warn-box"><strong>Effects on living tissue:</strong> Cell death, mutations (DNA damage), cancer. α is most ionising but least penetrating (dangerous if inhaled/ingested). γ penetrates deeply (most dangerous externally).</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- TOPIC 6 -->
<div class="page" id="page-topic6">
  <div class="section-title">🌌 <span>Topic 6: Space Physics</span></div>
  <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag tag-teal">Supplement Rich</span><span class="tag tag-purple">Hubble Constant</span><span class="tag tag-blue">Star Life Cycle</span></div>
  <div class="notes-container">
    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">6.1 Earth &amp; Solar System <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <ul><li>Earth: rotates on tilted axis ~24 h → day/night cycle</li>
          <li>Earth orbits Sun ~365 days → seasons (due to tilted axis)</li>
          <li>Moon orbits Earth ~1 month → phases of Moon</li>
          <li>Planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars | Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune</li>
          <li>Inner 4: rocky, small (too hot for gases to be retained)</li>
          <li>Outer 4: gaseous, large (cool enough to retain gas)</li>
          <li>Accretion model: gravity pulls rotating gas/dust cloud together → disc forms → planets coalesce</li>
          <li>Sun holds ~99.8% of Solar System mass → gravitational force keeps planets orbiting</li></ul>
          <h4>Gravitational Field</h4>
          <ul><li>Strength at surface depends on mass of planet</li>
          <li>Decreases with distance from planet</li>
          <li>Keeps moons in orbit around planets; planets around Sun</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Orbital speed (Supp)</span>v = 2πr / T</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement:</strong> Planets closer to Sun orbit faster (gravitational field stronger). Planets have elliptical orbits (Sun not at centre). Object in elliptical orbit travels faster when closer to Sun (conservation of energy: GPE converts to KE).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtopic-block">
      <div class="subtopic-header" onclick="toggleBlock(this)">6.2 Stars &amp; the Universe ★ HIGH YIELD <span class="subtopic-toggle">▼</span></div>
      <div class="subtopic-body">
        <div class="notes-text">
          <h4>The Sun</h4>
          <ul><li>Medium-sized star; mostly hydrogen and helium</li>
          <li>Radiates mainly infrared, visible light, UV</li>
          <li>Energy from nuclear fusion: H → He (powered by nuclear reactions)</li></ul>
          <h4>Stellar Life Cycle</h4>
          <ul><li><strong>Nebula:</strong> interstellar cloud of gas (mainly H) and dust</li>
          <li><strong>Protostar:</strong> cloud contracts under gravity → temperature rises</li>
          <li><strong>Stable star (main sequence):</strong> gravity IN balanced by radiation pressure OUT; H fuses to He</li>
          <li>When H exhausted → star expands</li>
          <li><strong>Average mass star:</strong> Red Giant → planetary nebula → White Dwarf (no fusion, cools)</li>
          <li><strong>Massive star:</strong> Red Supergiant → Supernova (explosion, creates heavy elements) → Neutron Star or Black Hole</li>
          <li>Supernova remnant (nebula) may form new stars with orbiting planets</li></ul>
          <h4>The Universe</h4>
          <ul><li>Milky Way: ~200 billion stars, diameter ~100,000 light-years</li>
          <li>Universe: billions of galaxies</li>
          <li>1 light-year = 9.5 × 10¹⁵ m (distance light travels in 1 year)</li>
          <li>Redshift: wavelength of light from receding galaxies is longer than emitted wavelength</li>
          <li>More distant galaxies → greater redshift → moving away faster</li>
          <li>Redshift = evidence that Universe is EXPANDING → supports Big Bang Theory</li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Hubble's Law (Supp)</span>H₀ = v / d</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><span class="formula-label">Age of Universe (Supp)</span>t ≈ 1 / H₀  ≈ 14 billion years</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — CMBR:</strong> Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation — microwave radiation detected uniformly from all directions in space. Produced shortly after Big Bang; expanded (redshifted) from higher frequency into microwave region as Universe expanded. Evidence for Big Bang.</div>
        <div class="supp-box"><strong>Supplement — Hubble Constant:</strong> H₀ = 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹. The distance to a far galaxy can be found using brightness of supernovae. Age of Universe ≈ 1/H₀.</div>
